::ENG2_U06_UA_Q01::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q01
[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which presentation claim is clearest for school leaders? \{
~A. School has some problems. #Incorrect. This is too vague.
=B. The school should provide a supervised after-school study space for students who remain on campus. #Correct. This claim states a clear proposal for a specific audience.
~C. People need stuff after school. #Incorrect. This is informal and unclear.
~D. My slides are about many things. #Incorrect. This lacks focus.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q02::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q02
[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which evidence best supports the claim? \{
~A. Students probably need somewhere to go. #Incorrect. This is a guess.
~B. The presenter likes the idea. #Incorrect. This is personal opinion.
~C. A study space sounds nice. #Incorrect. This is not evidence.
=D. In a survey of 120 students, 68 reported staying on campus after dismissal at least twice a week. #Correct. This is specific and credible survey evidence.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q03::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q03
[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which slide title best presents a finding? \{
=A. Survey Evidence Shows Many Students Stay After School #Correct. This title identifies the source and finding.
~B. Things We Found #Incorrect. This is vague.
~C. Our Presentation #Incorrect. This does not state a finding.
~D. Random Data #Incorrect. This makes the evidence sound unorganized.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q04::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q04
[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which speaker note best explains evidence? \{
~A. This number is here because it is important. #Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. The slide already says it. #Incorrect. The speaker should explain the evidence.
=C. Because more than half of surveyed students remain after school, the proposal addresses a need that affects many students. #Correct. This explains what the evidence means and connects it to the claim.
~D. I found a number in the survey. #Incorrect. This identifies source but not meaning.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q05::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q05
[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which sentence uses the best tone? \{
=A. The proposal would provide students with a supervised academic workspace after dismissal. #Correct. This is formal and precise.
~B. This would be a cool place for kids. #Incorrect. This is casual.
~C. People should just make it happen. #Incorrect. This is demanding and not academic.
~D. The thing is good. #Incorrect. This is vague.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q06::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q06
[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence is grammatically correct? \{
~A. Students needs a supervised study space. #Incorrect. The plural subject needs "need."
~B. Because students need a supervised study space. #Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~C. Students need, a supervised study space. #Incorrect. The comma is unnecessary.
=D. Students need a supervised study space. #Correct. The subject and verb agree.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q07::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q07
[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which revision best fits the audience of school leaders? \{
~A. Kids hang around and need a place. #Incorrect. This is too informal.
=B. Students who remain after dismissal need a supervised place to complete academic work. #Correct. This is precise, formal, and audience-aware.
~C. This thing would help people. #Incorrect. This is vague.
~D. Everyone knows this should happen. #Incorrect. This is unsupported.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q08::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q08
[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which visual design choice best supports the evidence? \{
~A. Tiny text with no label #Incorrect. The audience may not understand the data.
~B. A decorative image covering the chart #Incorrect. This hides evidence.
=C. A readable chart with a clear title, labels, and source context #Correct. This helps the audience understand the evidence.
~D. Multiple unrelated animations #Incorrect. These distract from the message.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q09::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q09
[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which final review step is most important before submitting? \{
~A. Check only the background color. #Incorrect. Readiness requires more than design.
~B. Delete the speaker notes. #Incorrect. The oral component matters.
~C. Ignore feedback. #Incorrect. Feedback should guide revision.
=D. Check that the claim, evidence, slide, speaker note, tone, and conventions align. #Correct. This confirms the presentation is ready to share.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q10::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q10
[MLA.ENG2.C.01] What is the purpose of an oral explanation? \{
=A. To help the audience understand how evidence supports the message #Correct. The speaker interprets evidence and guides understanding.
~B. To repeat every slide word exactly #Incorrect. Oral explanation should add reasoning.
~C. To avoid discussing evidence #Incorrect. Evidence should be explained.
~D. To make the presentation unrelated to the slide #Incorrect. Oral and digital parts should align.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q11::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q11
[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Why should survey size be included with the result? \{
~A. It makes the slide look longer. #Incorrect. Length is not the purpose.
~B. It replaces explanation. #Incorrect. Evidence still needs explanation.
=C. It tells the audience how many people provided the data. #Correct. This helps the audience judge the evidence.
~D. It hides the source. #Incorrect. It gives more source context.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q12::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q12
[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which slide problem should be fixed first? \{
~A. The slide has a clear title. #Incorrect. That is a strength, not a problem.
=B. The slide has important numbers in tiny unreadable text. #Correct. Evidence must be readable.
~C. The slide labels the chart. #Incorrect. Labels help understanding.
~D. The slide uses readable font. #Incorrect. Readability is a strength.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q13::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q13
[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which revision improves formal tone? \{
=A. Replace "awesome idea" with "effective proposal." #Correct. This changes casual wording into formal academic wording.
~B. Replace "proposal" with "thing." #Incorrect. This is less precise.
~C. Replace "evidence" with "stuff." #Incorrect. This is informal.
~D. Replace "students" with "kids hanging around." #Incorrect. This is less formal.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q14::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q14
[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence correctly uses a comma after an introductory phrase? \{
~A. After dismissal many students, remain on campus. #Incorrect. The comma is misplaced.
~B. After, dismissal many students remain on campus. #Incorrect. This splits the phrase incorrectly.
~C. After dismissal many students remain, on campus. #Incorrect. This comma is unnecessary.
=D. After dismissal, many students remain on campus. #Correct. The comma correctly follows the introductory phrase.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q15::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q15
[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Feedback says the presentation does not explain why evidence matters. What should be revised? \{
~A. Only the slide color #Incorrect. Color does not explain evidence.
~B. The file name only #Incorrect. This does not improve the presentation.
=C. The speaker note, so it interprets the evidence and connects it to the claim #Correct. This directly addresses the feedback.
~D. The evidence by deleting it #Incorrect. Evidence should be explained, not removed.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q16::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q16
[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which presentation is publication-ready? \{
~A. A presentation with clear slides but unrelated speaker notes #Incorrect. The parts must align.
=B. A presentation with aligned slides, clear evidence, formal notes, correct conventions, and feedback-based revision #Correct. This meets final readiness expectations.
~C. A presentation with evidence but many grammar errors #Incorrect. Conventions matter.
~D. A presentation with formal notes but unreadable slides #Incorrect. Digital support must be clear.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q17::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q17
[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which transition best connects evidence to reasoning? \{
~A. Anyway #Incorrect. This is informal and does not show reasoning.
~B. Randomly #Incorrect. This does not create a logical connection.
=C. This evidence matters because #Correct. This phrase signals that the speaker will explain the importance of the evidence.
~D. Whatever #Incorrect. This is informal and dismissive.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q18::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q18
[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Why should slides avoid full paragraphs of speaker text? \{
=A. Full paragraphs can overload the audience and weaken the role of oral explanation. #Correct. Slides should highlight evidence while the speaker explains.
~B. The audience should not see any information. #Incorrect. The audience should see key information.
~C. Evidence should always be hidden. #Incorrect. Evidence should be clear.
~D. Paragraphs automatically prove the claim. #Incorrect. Reasoning and evidence prove claims.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q19::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q19
[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which statement correctly uses evidence? \{
~A. The proposal is good because I like it. #Incorrect. This is opinion.
=B. Since 68 of 120 surveyed students stay after school, the need affects a large portion of respondents. #Correct. This uses specific evidence and explains meaning.
~C. Students need help because maybe they do. #Incorrect. This is uncertain.
~D. Everyone already knows the answer. #Incorrect. This is unsupported.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q20::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q20
[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence is a fragment? \{
~A. Students remain after school. #Incorrect. This is complete.
~B. The proposal supports students. #Incorrect. This is complete.
~C. The survey result matters. #Incorrect. This is complete.
=D. Because the survey result matters. #Correct. This dependent clause does not complete the thought.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q21::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q21
[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which response best uses collaborator feedback? \{
=A. Feedback said the note was vague, so I revised it to explain how the evidence supports the proposal. #Correct. This applies feedback to a specific communication problem.
~B. Feedback said the note was vague, so I ignored it. #Incorrect. This does not improve the work.
~C. Feedback said the note was vague, so I deleted the evidence. #Incorrect. This weakens the presentation.
~D. Feedback said the note was vague, so I changed an unrelated color. #Incorrect. This does not address the issue.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q22::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q22
[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which revision best improves precision? \{
~A. Replace "students" with "people." #Incorrect. This is less precise.
~B. Replace "evidence" with "stuff." #Incorrect. This is vague.
=C. Replace "a place" with "a supervised academic workspace." #Correct. This makes the wording more specific.
~D. Replace "proposal" with "thing." #Incorrect. This is less precise.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q23::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q23
[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which speaker note best fits a closing? \{
~A. That's all I have. #Incorrect. This does not reinforce the message.
~B. I am done now. #Incorrect. This is not a strong academic closing.
~C. The end, I guess. #Incorrect. This is informal and uncertain.
=D. For these reasons, the proposal would address a documented student need after school. #Correct. This closing reinforces the claim and evidence.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q24::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q24
[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which slide and note pairing is strongest? \{
~A. Slide: Need. Note: This is important and stuff. #Incorrect. Both parts are vague.
=B. Slide: 68 of 120 surveyed students stay after school. Note: This result shows the need affects many students. #Correct. The slide provides evidence, and the note explains it.
~C. Slide: Random image. Note: Survey evidence matters. #Incorrect. The slide does not support the note.
~D. Slide: Full script. Note: Same full script. #Incorrect. This overloads the slide and repeats instead of explaining.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q25::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q25
[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which final statement best describes Unit 6 mastery? \{
=A. I can present a clear message using oral explanation, digital support, credible evidence, revision, and formal language. #Correct. This captures the full Unit 6 synthesis.
~B. I can make colorful slides without evidence. #Incorrect. Evidence and explanation are required.
~C. I can read slides without explaining them. #Incorrect. Oral explanation matters.
~D. I can submit without checking conventions. #Incorrect. Final work must be reviewed.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q26::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q26
[MLA.ENG2.C.01] A student is speaking to school leaders about after-school study space. Which opening best fits the audience?\{
~A. You all need to fix this right now.#Incorrect. This is demanding and not appropriate for school leaders.
~B. The hallway is annoying and everybody knows it.#Incorrect. This is informal and vague.
=C. Thank you for considering how a supervised study space could support students who remain on campus after dismissal.#Correct. This opening is respectful, clear, and audience-aware.
~D. I just want a better place because I said so.#Incorrect. This is personal and unsupported.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q27::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q27
[MLA.ENG2.C.01] What is the speaker's purpose if the task says, "Explain why students need a quiet place after school"?\{
~A. To entertain listeners with a funny story.#Incorrect. The task asks for explanation, not entertainment.
=B. To explain a student need to listeners.#Correct. The speaker's purpose is to explain why the study space is needed.
~C. To describe every room in the school.#Incorrect. That would not match the assigned purpose.
~D. To avoid sharing a perspective.#Incorrect. Oral communication should present a clear perspective.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q28::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q28
[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which phrase uses the most appropriate academic vocabulary?\{
=A. a supervised academic space#Correct. This phrase is precise and appropriate for school communication.
~B. a cool spot#Incorrect. This is informal.
~C. a good thing#Incorrect. This is vague.
~D. some stuff after school#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q29::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q29
[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which oral outline order is clearest for listeners?\{
~A. Evidence, closing, opening, random fact, claim.#Incorrect. This order is confusing.
~B. Closing, claim, opening, evidence, reasoning.#Incorrect. Starting with the closing weakens organization.
~C. Random facts, claim, no explanation, ending.#Incorrect. Random facts and no reasoning do not support clear oral communication.
=D. Opening, claim, evidence, reasoning, closing.#Correct. This order helps listeners follow the speaker's point.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q30::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q30
[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which source detail is most credible evidence?\{
=A. A survey of 120 students reports that 68 students remain on campus after dismissal at least one day each week.#Correct. This is specific evidence from a stated survey.
~B. My friend says hallways are bad.#Incorrect. This is a personal comment without source support.
~C. Everybody knows students wait around.#Incorrect. This is vague and unsupported.
~D. I think a study space sounds nice.#Incorrect. This is an opinion, not evidence.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q31::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q31
[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which perspective is most focused for a presentation to school leaders?\{
~A. The library has books, snacks are good, and hallways exist.#Incorrect. This mixes unrelated ideas.
~B. I like many things about school.#Incorrect. This is too broad and personal.
=C. A supervised after-school study space would help students work safely while waiting on campus.#Correct. This is clear, focused, and audience-appropriate.
~D. Students do homework sometimes and also read novels.#Incorrect. This is not a focused perspective.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q32::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q32
[MLA.ENG2.R.07] Why should a speaker remove a detail about favorite library books from this presentation?\{
~A. Books are always bad evidence.#Incorrect. Books can be relevant in other presentations.
~B. It uses too many numbers.#Incorrect. The detail does not use numbers.
~C. It is the strongest safety evidence.#Incorrect. It does not address safety or study space.
=D. It does not directly support the purpose of explaining after-school study space.#Correct. The detail distracts from the presentation's purpose.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q33::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q33
[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which slide heading best states a finding?\{
~A. Survey#Incorrect. This names a source type but not the finding.
=B. Many Students Remain on Campus After Dismissal#Correct. This heading tells the audience what the evidence shows.
~C. Stuff About School#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~D. Slide Two#Incorrect. This labels position, not meaning.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q34::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q34
[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which visual feature best helps the audience compare survey numbers?\{
~A. A busy background image.#Incorrect. This distracts from the numbers.
~B. Moving text on every line.#Incorrect. This can distract the audience.
=C. A simple labeled bar chart.#Correct. A bar chart helps the audience compare quantities.
~D. A decorative border with no labels.#Incorrect. Decoration does not clarify the evidence.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q35::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q35
[MLA.ENG2.C.02] A slide heading says, "Stuff We Found." Which revision best improves clarity for a digital presentation about the student survey? \{
=A. Survey Evidence Shows Students Need Supervised Space #Correct. This heading tells the audience the topic, the evidence source, and the main finding. A clear heading prepares the viewer to understand the slide.
~B. Survey Things #Incorrect. This heading is still vague. It does not tell what the survey showed or why the information matters.
~C. Our Project #Incorrect. This heading identifies that a project exists, but it does not help the audience understand the specific finding.
~D. A Colorful Student Slide #Incorrect. This focuses on appearance instead of the information the audience needs to understand.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q36::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q36
[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which detail is the strongest evidence to include on a slide about the need for an after-school study space? \{
~A. Some students probably stay after school. #Incorrect. This is a guess. Digital presentations need evidence that can be traced to a source or data point.
~B. Everyone knows students like places to study. #Incorrect. This is a broad claim without support. It does not provide verifiable evidence.
~C. A study space would be nice. #Incorrect. This is an opinion, not evidence. It does not show why the solution is needed.
=D. 68 of 120 surveyed students remain on campus after dismissal. #Correct. This is specific, measurable evidence. It helps the audience understand the size of the need.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q37::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q37
[MLA.ENG2.C.01] A slide says, "68 of 120 students remain on campus after dismissal." Which speaker note best explains the evidence? \{
~A. This number is here and it is kind of important. #Incorrect. This note is vague. It does not explain the meaning or importance of the evidence.
=B. This result matters because more than half of the surveyed students stay after school, so the campus has a real need for a supervised study space. #Correct. The note explains what the number means and connects it to the presentation purpose.
~C. The slide already says the number, so I do not need to explain it. #Incorrect. Oral communication should interpret important evidence, not simply point to it.
~D. I found a number and put it on the slide. #Incorrect. This note focuses on the presenter's action instead of explaining the evidence to the audience.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q38::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q38
[MLA.ENG2.C.02] In an integrated oral and digital presentation, what should the slide and speaker note do together? \{
=A. The slide should show the key evidence, and the speaker note should explain the reasoning. #Correct. This creates a clear partnership between visual support and oral explanation.
~B. The slide should include every word the speaker plans to say. #Incorrect. A slide overloaded with text is hard to follow and weakens oral delivery.
~C. The slide should be decorative, and the speaker should provide all information. #Incorrect. A digital slide should clarify information, not serve only as decoration.
~D. The slide and speaker note should repeat the exact same sentence. #Incorrect. Repetition does not add explanation. The speaker should deepen the audience's understanding.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q39::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q39
[MLA.ENG2.C.01] During rehearsal, a student notices the speaker note says only, "This is good." Which revision best improves oral explanation? \{
~A. This is good and everyone should like it. #Incorrect. This is still vague and too casual for a formal presentation.
~B. The slide is blue and has a title. #Incorrect. This describes appearance instead of explaining the message.
~C. I rehearsed and then stopped. #Incorrect. This does not revise the oral explanation.
=D. This proposal would support students who remain after dismissal by giving them a supervised place to complete academic work. #Correct. This revision explains the proposal, the audience need, and the reason clearly.
}



::ENG2_U06_UA_Q40::
Question ID: ENG2_U06_UA_Q40
[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Feedback says the chart is hard to understand. Which revision best addresses the feedback? \{
~A. Add a brighter background behind the chart. #Incorrect. A brighter background may make the data harder to read.
~B. Remove the evidence and keep the colors. #Incorrect. Removing evidence weakens the presentation.
=C. Add a clear chart title, labels, and readable numbers. #Correct. This directly improves the audience's ability to understand the digital evidence.
~D. Read the chart faster during the presentation. #Incorrect. Speed does not solve unclear visual design.
}
